The real fix for the mamba-align prefill self-preemption bug documented
in docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md. Replaces the earlier debug-only
patch of the same file (preemption-mamba-align-freeing-debug-logging.patch,
now removed -- its logging proved the mechanism and is no longer needed).

Root cause: `mamba_cache_mode="align"` tracked exactly one "block index
superseded by a newer running-state block" per request, in a plain
scalar (`last_state_block_idx: dict[str, int]`). Every step that
allocates a new running-state block OVERWRITES that scalar with the
new stale position -- unconditionally, whether or not the *previous*
stale position had actually been freed yet. During a continuous
chunked-prefill run, the freeing check (`last_state_block_idx <
processed-tokens-derived threshold`) stays in permanent lockstep --
equal, never strictly less -- for the entire prefill (proven via direct
instrumentation: 0/107 successful frees across 3 Mamba/GDN KV-cache
groups, at two different chunk sizes). So the scalar gets overwritten,
over and over, before its check ever passes, silently dropping every
stale block except the very latest -- and none of them ever get freed
during prefill. Confirmed with a shared-pool full-attention manager, the
model's 3 separate Mamba/GDN groups leak ~1 never-freed block per group
per scheduling step, which is what exhausts the KV cache pool and
self-preempts a single, uncontested large-prompt request against nothing
but its own bookkeeping.

The fix: turn the scalar into a per-request FIFO queue
(`_stale_state_block_idxs: dict[str, list[int]]`). Nothing about *when*
a position becomes safe to free changes -- same threshold, same
comparison, same GPU-synchronization safety margin (deliberately not
touched: processed_computed_tokens excludes tokens whose GPU-side work
isn't confirmed complete yet, and loosening that comparison risked a
real correctness bug, not just a memory leak). What changes is that
every pending entry gets evaluated, not only the newest. Since indices
are strictly increasing and the newest one sits exactly at the
threshold every step (proven above), every *older* pending entry is
therefore already strictly past the threshold and gets freed
immediately -- the queue stays bounded at ~1 pending entry instead of
growing with the prompt.

Verified (2026-08-21/22): the same 88K-token solo-prompt repro that
self-preempted at --max-num-batched-tokens=2048 (peak GPU-KV usage
99.6%) now peaks at 54.9% with zero preemptions, at the same batch
size -- and throughput actually improved (885 vs ~860 tok/s, since this
also removes the reason --max-num-batched-tokens had to be dropped to
1024 as a workaround). Correctness spot-checked with a needle-in-
haystack recall test (unique code embedded ~44K tokens into an 88K-token
prompt, greedy decoding) -- correctly recalled.

